y":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in: v4.6.2.stable.arch_linux [001aa128b], v4.7.beta1.official [1c8cc9e7e]\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.7.beta1 - CachyOS Linux #1 SMP PREEMPT Fri, 01 May 2026 19:46:26 +0000 on Wayland - Wayland display driver, Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Mobile) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4060 Laptop GPU - AMD Ryzen 7 7735HS with Radeon Graphics (16 threads) - 14.86 GiB memory - PulseAudio (44100 Hz, Stereo/mono)\n\n### Issue description\n\nIf game embedding is enabled switching your focus from the embedded game window to an input entry in the editor often makes the input field behave as if both NumLock and CapsLock are turned off, even when that is not actually the case. Toggling these on/off or clicking around fixes the issue, but it still interrupts the flow.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Enable \"prefer Wayland\" in editor settings\n2. Open or create a project that could be ran (a scene with a Node3D as its root is enough)\n3. Enable game embedding\n4. Turn on NumLock and/or CapsLock\n5. Select a node with editable properties, verify that inputs behave as expected\n6. Start the game\n7. Click on its embedded window\n8. Click on an input field in editor and observe that NumLock/CapsLock behave as if they are off\n\nYou might have to repeat steps 7-8 a few times (not sure why), but it does happen very often in my experience.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A, reproducible with a trivial project.","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/starre